NAME
     grep - print the lines that match a pattern

SYNOPSIS
     grep [-EFivnclLqswxohHr] [-m NUM] [-e PATTERN] PATTERN [FILE...]
     egrep ...   fgrep ...   rgrep ...

DESCRIPTION
     grep prints the lines of each FILE, or of standard input, that match
     PATTERN. The pattern is a basic regular expression; -E makes it an
     extended one and -F a plain string. Called as egrep, fgrep or rgrep
     it starts as though -E, -F or -r had been given.

OPTIONS
     -E   extended regular expressions
     -F   fixed strings
     -i   ignore case
     -v   the lines that do not match
     -n   put the line number in front of each line
     -c   only the count
     -l   only the names of the files that match
     -L   only the names of the files that do not
     -q   print nothing: the exit status is the answer
     -s   say nothing about files that cannot be read
     -w   match whole words
     -x   match whole lines
     -o   print only the part that matched
     -h   never print the file name
     -H   always print the file name
     -r   look under directories
     -m NUM   stop after NUM matching lines per file
     -e PATTERN   the pattern, for one that begins with a dash

SEE ALSO
     sed(1), awk(1), find(1)
